  "image_name": "712cabfc35c551161ad229dfa207df38.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how carbon is stored and moved across an Arctic land–river–ocean system, with emphasis on how climate change is altering those exchanges. It shows large carbon stocks locked in terrestrial soils and permafrost, with additional stocks in marine waters and marine sediments. Arrows indicate major fluxes linking land, rivers, coasts, sediments, the ocean, and the atmosphere.\n\nThe overall message is that warming is mobilizing previously frozen carbon and changing source–sink behavior. On land, permafrost thaw, soil collapse, and melting glaciers appear to increase the release and transport of carbon. Rivers then carry this material seaward as dissolved and particulate forms. Along the coast, erosion transfers soil organic carbon into the ocean, where mineralization, resuspension, gas release, and sediment burial redistribute it further.\n\nThe marine side is also shown as dynamic: there is exchange between ocean and atmosphere, primary production in surface waters, and transfer to sediments below. Additional labels point to sea-ice retreat, ocean acidification and freshening, and Arctic greening, suggesting broad environmental shifts that affect the carbon cycle. The orange annotations highlight the big-picture interpretation: more active carbon cycling, changing source and sink dynamics, and increasingly non-linear change with more extreme responses.",
